raises or ref.adds... The task says D4 currently writes a record, so `H.read_log` doesn't refuse this fixture. Good.\n\nBut wait: currently, `evaluator_history` is called and `H.read_log` reads the single-object log. It returns `log_calls`. Then `calls = [c for c in log_calls if c.at]`. If `H.read_log` returned an empty list (can't parse object), then `calls` empty \u2192 `evaluator_history` returns `{}`. Then `ev_calls[\"evaluator\"] = {}`. Then link_call returns None (no calls). But my module entry would still be found (if I call read separately). So I can set the check time from my module even if `H.read_log` returned nothing. \n\nBut then `evaluator_history` returns `{}` and `eh.get(\"entries\")` wouldn't be set if I only set it in the non-empty branch. Let me set entries regardless, before the `if not calls: return {}` check. Actually, let me restructure `evaluator_history` to read with my module first and attach entries.\n\nLet me write the new `evaluator_history`:\n\n```python\ndef evaluator_history(job: Path, ev_dir: Path, ci: dict, e: dict, ref: H.Refusals, pin: dict | None = None,\n                      gaps: Gaps | None = None, hand: dict | None = None) -> dict:\n    who = f\"evaluator {e.get('id')}\"\n    rel = e.get(\"harness_log\") or next((x.get(\"evidence_file\") for x in ci.get(\"external\") or []\n                                        if x.get(\"subject\") == e.get(\"id\")), None)\n    log = find_log(job, ev_dir, rel, None)\n    if log is None:\n        ref.add(\"time\", f\"{who}: a model evaluator needs its harness log (evaluators[].harness_log, or the CL1-15 \"\n                        f\"external entry for it); {'not found: ' + repr(rel) if rel else 'none named'}, so its check \"\n                        f\"times and endpoint have no source\")\n        return {}\n    # ONE reading of the log (kit/evaluator_harness_log.py): a malformed, partial or reversed entry is refused\n    # here, naming the entry, so no check time is taken from a log the record cannot honestly read.\n    try:\n        entries = harness_log.read(log)\n    except harness_log.HarnessLogError as exc:\n        ref.add(\"time\", f\"{who}: {exc}\")\n        return {}\n    ...\n    out = {...}\n    out[\"entries\"] = entries\n    return out\n```\n\nWait, the module name is `evaluator_harness_log` (file `kit/evaluator_harness_log.py`). I'll import it as `harness_log`.\n\nBut careful: `ref.add(\"time\", f\"{who}: {exc}\")` \u2192 message includes \"evaluator evaluator: entry 'dim1#p1' in harness-eval.log: received_at ... is before sent_at ...\". D8 asserts \"dim1#p1\" in output. Good. D9 similar.\n\nD6: log is None \u2192 existing message \"needs its harness log\". Good. D11: kind command \u2192 not called. Good.\n\nNow, `H.read_log` still called for endpoint stuff. But if the log is a single object and `H.read_log` chokes... it might raise an unhandled exception? The task says D4 currently works (writes record), so it doesn't raise. But to be safe, I could wrap `H.read_log` in try/except? Hmm, but that changes behavior. Actually, if `H.read_log` returns empty, then `calls` empty \u2192 `return {}`. But I want to still provide entries. So let me handle: read entries first, then `H.read_log`. If `H.read_log` returns no calls, I still return `{\"entries\": entries, \"calls\": {}, ...}`? But `out` currently requires `first = min(calls, ...)`. If calls empty, return `{}`. But then entries lost.\n\nLet me restructure: if `H.read_log` yields no calls but my module has entries, still return `{\"entries\": entries, \"calls\": {}}` so the check can use the module entry. But `out[\"endpoint\"]` etc. absent. That's fine; build uses `.get`.\n\nActually, let me keep it simpler and robust:\n\n```python\n    log_calls = H.read_log(log, ref, who)\n    flag_calls(log_calls, hand)\n    calls = [c for c in log_calls if c.at]\n    entries = ...\n    if not calls:\n        return {\"entries\": entries, \"calls\": {}, \"endpoint\": None, \"x\": route_x(log, log_ca