ld otherwise be a silent hole.\n        pfacts_all = ci.get(\"passes\") or {}\n        passes_src = [{\"pass_id\": pid} for pid in pfacts_all]\n```\n\nWait, `pfacts` is defined later: `pfacts = ci.get(\"passes\") or {}`. Let me place the fallback where `pfacts` is available, or move. The `passes_src` list is used at `for p in passes_src`. `pfacts` is defined just before that loop. Let me add the fallback right before the loop:\n\n```python\n    pfacts = ci.get(\"passes\") or {}\n    if not passes_src and not rec:\n        passes_src = [{\"pass_id\": pid} for pid in pfacts]\n```\n\nBut `rec` might be truthy but `passes_src` empty (if \u00a75 record has no passes). Then don't fall back \u2014 because a \u00a75 record with no passes means no filed passes. Use `not rec`. Good.\n\nHmm, but what if `rec` exists but has passes, and also pfacts has extra passes? Keep \u00a75 authority. Fine.\n\nNow let me reconsider the new module's handling of a single-object log combined with `H.read_log`. Actually, let me test mentally D4 with `H.read_log` reading a single object. If `H.read_log` internally does `data = json.loads(text)` and then iterates `for i, e in enumerate(data)` where data is a dict \u2192 iterating a dict yields keys (strings), then `e.get` on a string \u2192 AttributeError \u2192 exception propagates out of `evaluator_history` \u2192 caught in `main`? `main` wraps `build` in `try: ... except (ValueError, OSError)`. AttributeError not caught \u2192 crash \u2192 returncode non-zero, no record \u2192 D4 fails!\n\nBut the task says D4 currently writes a record. So `H.read_log` must handle a dict gracefully (maybe it checks `isinstance(data, list)` and wraps, or if dict wraps). Since I can't see it, and the task says D4 writes a record, I'll trust it handles the dict. But if it doesn't, the current D4 wouldn't write a record, contradicting the task. So it does.\n\nActually, wait: maybe `H.read_log` raises and the emitter catches... no. Let me just trust.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtle thing: I'm calling `harness_log.read(log)` which I control. Then `H.read_log`. If `H.read_log` crashes on dict, the whole thing crashes. But since D4 currently writes, it's fine.\n\nNow, another consideration: `evaluator_history` currently calls `H.read_log` then `flag_calls`. If `H.read_log` returns empty (dict not handled but tolerated), then `calls` empty \u2192 `return {}`. My module entries are computed before that, but `out` returns without entries. Let me ensure I attach entries even when calls empty.\n\nLet me write the new `evaluator_history` carefully.\n\nCurrent code:\n\n```python\ndef evaluator_history(job: Path, ev_dir: Path, ci: dict, e: dict, ref: H.Refusals, pin: dict | None = None,\n                      gaps: Gaps | None = None, hand: dict | None = None) -> dict:\n    who = f\"evaluator {e.get('id')}\"\n    rel = e.get(\"harness_log\") or next((x.get(\"evidence_file\") for x in ci.get(\"external\") or []\n                                        if x.get(\"subject\") == e.get(\"id\")), None)\n    log = find_log(job, ev_dir, rel, None)\n    if log is None:\n        ref.add(\"time\", f\"{who}: a model evaluator needs its harness log (evaluators[].harness_log, or the CL1-15 \"\n                        f\"external entry for it); {'not found: ' + repr(rel) if rel else 'none named'}, so its check \"\n                        f\"times and endpoint have no source\")\n        return {}\n    # The emitter never writes its inputs (T7): the log is read as it is. Every call counts for T7b, not only the\n    # call a check is linked to.\n    log_calls = H.read_log(log, ref, who)\n    flag_calls(log_calls, hand)\n    calls = [c for c in log_calls if c.at]\n    if not calls:\n        return {}\n    annotate(calls, who, (e.get(\"model\") or {}).get(\"identifier\"), ref, pin)\n    check_pin_order(pin, calls, who, ref)\n    first = min(calls, key=lambda c: c.at)\n    hand_ep = (e.get(\"model\") or {}).get(\"endpoint\")\n    if hand_ep and first.endpoint and hand_ep != first.endpoint:\n        ref.add(\"endpoint\", f\"evaluators[{e.get('id')}].model.endpoint: hand-entered {hand_ep!r} but {first.label} \"\n                            + (f\"went to {first.host} and was answered upstream by \"\n                               f\"{first.route.get('upstream_provider')!r} ({first.endpoint})\"\n                               if first.route.get(\"aggregator\") else f\"was answered by {first.endpoint}\"))\n    named = {}\n    for c in calls:\n        named.setdefault(c.name or str(c.i), c)\n    x = route_x(log, calls)\n    rx = routing_x(calls, pin, str(e.get(\"id\")), gaps if gaps is not None else Gaps())\n    if rx:\n        x[\"routing\"] = rx\n    return {\"calls\": named, \"endpoint\": first.endpoint, \"x\": x}\n```\n\nI'll insert after `log = find_log(...)` block:\n\n```python\n    # ONE reading of the log (kit/evaluator_harness_log.py) BEFORE any call is linked to a check: a malformed,\n    # partial or reversed entry is refused here, naming the entry, so no check time is taken from a log the\n    # record cannot honestly read. The existing per-call reading below still supplies endpoints and routes.\n    try:\n        entries = harness_log.read(log)\n    except harness_log.HarnessLogError as exc:\n        ref.add(\"time\", f\"{who}: {exc}\")\n        return {}\n```\n\nThen at the end, include `\"entries\": entries`. And for the early `if not calls: return {}`, change to `return {\"entries\": entries, \"calls\": {}, \"endpoint\": None, \"x\": route_x(log, log_calls)}`. Hmm, but route_x expects calls; with empty list it returns `{\"harness_log\": log.name, \"calls\": []}`. Fine.\n\nActually, if `calls` is empty but entries exist, we still want the check time from entries. So return entries.\n\nLet me modify `if not calls:` block:\n\n```python\n    if not calls:\n        return {\"entries\": entries, \"calls\": {},\n                \"endpoint\": None, \"x\": {\"harness_log\": log.name, \"calls\": []}}\n```\n\nNow the end