ass. However `x-plumbline.times` etc. fine.\n\nBut careful: exit 0 means no HARD gaps. Hand-time allowance removes the refusal; hard gaps are things like missing bar digest. So stamping only eval log's logger times suffices to avoid the D6/D9 refusal, and exit 0 remains.\n\nBut hold on: the T7b/E3 case shows that when allow_hand=False FIX refuses with hand-time for eval log calls. After we add logger times to the eval log in our NEW fixture (a copy), FIX itself stays untouched (E3 depends on FIX being unstamped). Good \u2014 we copy FIX into a new job dir and stamp only the copy.\n\nNow, D8/D9: `harness_log.read` refuses reversed/partial. Our stamped entries: sent_at <= received_at needed. If we set both to the same instant, fine.\n\nNow, what instant? We must preserve `at` semantics for cross-checks. Let's think: what does `read()` produce as `received_at` when present? The parsed received_at timestamp. `at_v = H.iso(entry.received_at)` becomes the check's `at`. Cross-check `H.cross_check(ref, f\"passes.{pid}.at\", f.get(\"at\"), call.at, ...)` uses `call.at` from H.read_log \u2014 now = received_at = our value. If custody `passes.pass-a` has no `at` (E2's copy added it), then no cross-check on `at`. But what about `controls[*].at`? `link_call` then cross_check with `k.get(\"at\")` if set. If controls have typed `at` in the fixture and we set received_at to the call's *server* time \u2014 which already equals the typed `at` within tolerance (since currently emitting with hand times passes \u2014 wait, does it? `H.cross_check` is a refusal regardless of allow_hand; the current FIX run gives exit 0, so cross-checks currently pass). So using the call's current server-derived instant preserves the status quo exactly. \n\nSo: for each entry in the eval log, set sent_at = received_at = the instant the old reader derived (Date header or created). To compute that, use `kit/custody_history.py`'s `read_log`. But wait: does `read_log` skip entries lacking `at`? It returns Calls; `H.iso(c.at)`. If `c.at` is None \u2192 skip/error.\n\nActually, is using custody_history's read_log safe regarding \"who\"/refusals? `H.read_log(log, ref, who)` \u2014 signature per usage: `H.read_log(log, ref, \"maker\")`. It might add refusals for missing times? For the maker it's used to read; J: the maker's calls would be flagged as hand times by flag_calls (not read_log). Let me just use a dummy Refusals.\n\nAlternatively, simpler and dependency-free: parse the Date header ourselves with `email.utils.parsedate_to_datetime`, or the `created` epoch. But the precedence matters for exactness... Actually if both present and differ, `at` picks one. To be exact, use the emitter's reader.\n\nHmm, but there's another wrinkle: `harness_log.read` might not accept the same log shapes. The eval log's shape is what it is; read() is designed for it.\n\nAlso: the module `read()` returns entries keyed by `call`. For our copy, names unchanged.\n\nLet me now also consider: does `harness_log.read` require the log to be JSONL or JSON list? Unknown; it reads FIX's log (whatever format it is).\n\nImplementation plan:\n\n1. Add a helper near the other custody helpers (after `job_copy`/`append_text`? It's used at the emit-success section, which is after `job_copy`). Place it right before the emit-success block.\n\n```python\ndef logged_job(name):\n    \"\"\"FIX with the logger's own sent_at/received_at written into every call of its harness logs.\n\n    The fixture's logs carry server times only, so a model evaluator's log cannot be read (D6/D7:\n    the emitter refuses a model evaluator whose check times have no logged source) and every call\n    is a hand time. The entry shape is the frozen bar's (tests/test_evaluator_harness_log.py):\n    sent_at, received_at, the request url and the served model. The instants are the ones the\n    fixture already carries -- the response Date header or its created epoch -- so nothing the\n    fixture asserts about its own timeline moves.\"\"\"\n    d = TMP / name\n    shutil.copytree(FIX, d)\n    sys.path.insert(0, str(SNAP / \"kit\"))\n    try:\n        import custody_history as _hist\n    finally:\n        sys.path.pop(0)\n    for lg in sorted((d / \"custody\" / \"evidence\").glob(\"harness-*.log\")):\n        calls = _hist.read_log(lg, _hist.Refusals(), \"test\")\n        ... rewrite ...\n    return d\n```\n\nHmm, rewriting needs to know the file format (JSON list vs JSONL) and to map Call \u2192 raw dict. `Call` has `.i` (index) and `.raw`. If the log is a JSON list, `raw` is the entry dict; writing back the modified list preserves order by index. If JSONL, each line is an entry; `raw` is the parsed line; we can rewrite lines by index.\n\nBut `Call.raw` may be a *copy* or the actual dict? `self.raw = raw` \u2014 actual reference. If read_log parses the whole list into dicts, mutating c.raw mutates the doc. Then dumping the doc works. But we don't know whether read_log pre-processes (e.g., merges). Risky.\n\nSafer: do our own minimal, format-preserving transform:\n\n```python\ndef _with_logger_times(path):\n    text = path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    try:\n        doc = json.loads(text)\n    except ValueError:\n        doc = None\n    if isinstance(doc, list):\n        items, dump = doc, lambda it: json.dumps(it, indent=2) + \"\\n\"\n    else:\n        items = [json.loads(ln) for ln in text.splitlines() if ln.strip()]\n        dump = lambda it: \"\".join(json.dumps(e) + \"\\n\" for e in it)\n    for e in items:\n        if isinstance(e, dict) and not e.get(\"sent_at\") and not e.get(\"received_at\"):\n            at = _server_instant(e)\n            if at:\n                e[\"sent_at\"] = e[\"received_at\"] = at\n    path.write_text(dump(items), encoding=\"utf-8\")\n```\n\nHmm, but if the log is a single JSON object (not list) \u2014 like the same-family test that does `json.loads(lg.read_text())` and treats it as one call with `x[\"response\"]`! Look: \n\n```python\nj = job_copy(\"pin-attempt\")\nlg = j / \"custody\" / \"evidence\" / \"harness-maker-fixture.log\"; x = json.loads(lg.read_text())\nx[\"response\"][\"openrouter_metadata\"] = ...\n```\n\nSo `harness-maker-fixture.log` is a JSON object (a single call) or a dict wrapper. And `stamp_log` handles list/dict/JSONL:\n\n```python\ndoc = json.loads(text)\nitems, array = (doc, True) if isinstance(doc, list) else ([doc], False)\n...\npath.write_text((json.dumps(items, indent=2) + \"\\n\") if array\n                else \"\".join(json.dumps(c) + \"\\n\" for c in items), encoding=\"utf-8\")\n```\n\nSo `stamp_log` in this file already implements exactly this transform, with a fixed `at`. I can generalize it and move it up, or define a small variant.\n\nGiven the instruction \"reuse the shape\", and to minimize duplication, I'll move `stamp_log` (and `_iso`/`_ts` if needed) up before its new first use? Moving functions is allowed but requires care: `_iso` and `_ts` also used later. Simpler: define a *new* helper specifically for this in the custody section, using a per-entry server instant, and leave `stamp_log` alone.\n\nServer instant extraction:\n\n```python\ndef _server_instant(entry):\n    \"\"\"The instant a call's own response carried: the logger's times if present, else the response\n    `created` epoch, else the Date header. Same precedence as kit/custody_history.py.\"\"\"\n    for k in (\"sent_at\", \"received_at\"):\n        v = entry.get(k)\n        if isinstance(v, str) and v:\n            ...\n```\n\nHmm. Actually, precedence matters only for choosing what to stamp. Simplest and safest: use the *server* time (created or Date) rather than sent/received \u2014 that's what `at` resolves to when logger times are absent. Order: created first or Date first? From E3's sources: \"server-header\" or \"response-created\" \u2014 both possible. `hand_time_entries` reports the source. In H.Call, `self.at` likely = received or created or Date. Let me guess from `maker_history`: `\"startedAt_basis\": (f\"{first.label} sent_at\" if first.sent else (f\"{first.label} response created\" if first.created else f\"{first.label} response Date header\"))` \u2014 precedence: sent \u2192 created \u2192 Date. And `at_basis` for last: `f\"{last.label} received_at\" if last.received else f\"{last.label} {last.at_basis}\"` where at_basis \u2208 {response created, response Date header}. So `at` = received or created or Date header. Good: precedence created before Date.\n\nSo:\n\n```python\ndef _call_instant(e):\n    created = ((e.get(\"response\") or {}).get(\"created\"))\n    if isinstance(created, (int, float)) and not isinstance(created, bool):\n        return datetime.fromtimestamp(created, timezone.utc).strftime(\"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ\")\n    hdrs = e.get(\"response_headers\") or e.get(\"headers\") or (e.get(\"response\") or {}).get(\"headers\") or {}\n    date = hdrs.get(\"Date\") or hdrs.get(\"date\")\n    if isinstance(date, str) and date:\n        try:\n            from email.utils import parsedate_to_datetime\n            return parsedate_to_datetime(date).astimezone(timezone.utc).strftime(\"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ\")\n        except Exception:\n            return None\n    return None\n```\n\nHmm, `headers` in H.Call: `hdrs = raw.get(\"response_headers\") or raw.get(\"headers\") or {}` \u2014 so headers at top level of entry, not inside response. OK, include both.\n\nThen:\n\n```python\ndef logger_stamped_job(name):\n    d = TMP / name\n    shutil.copytree(FIX, d)\n    for lg in sorted((d / \"custody\" / \"evidence\").glob(\"harness-*.log\")):\n        text = lg.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\")\n        try:\n            doc = json.loads(text)\n        except ValueError:\n            doc = None\n        array = isinstance(doc, list)\n        items = doc if array else ([doc] if doc is not None else\n                                   [json.loads(ln) for ln in text.splitlines() if ln.strip()])\n        for e in items:\n            if not isinstance(e, dict) or e.get(\"sent_at\") or e.get(\"received_at\"):\n                continue\n            at = _call_instant(e)\n            if at:\n                e[\"sent_at\"] = e[\"received_at\"] = at\n        lg.write_text((json.dumps(items, indent=2) + \"\\n\") if array\n                      else \"\".join(json.dumps(e) + \"\\n\" for e in items), encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    return d\n```\n\nWait, careful with the dict case: `stamp_log` writes dict case as JSONL lines. Hmm, that changes format from single object to JSONL \u2014 but \"harness-maker-fixture.log\" was read by `json.loads` as a dict in tests after `stamp_log`? No \u2014 after `stamp_log`, the same-family test isn't rerun. But at the final T4 tool-built job, `stamp_log` rewrites all harness logs, converting dict \u2192 JSONL. Then `H.read_log` handles both. OK.\n\nBut wait: for the same-family test (line ~560) reading `json.loads(lg.read_text())` expecting a dict \u2014 that runs before T4 and on a fresh copy. Fine.\n\nFor our new fixture: if the maker log is a dict and we convert it to JSONL, does `H.read_log` handle it? Yes, since stamp_log does this for T4 and T4 passes (asserts exit 0). Good. But to be safer, preserve the dict form: if doc is a dict, write `json.dumps(doc, indent=2)`. Hmm, but `read_log`... it handled dict before, so preserving is safest. Let me preserve: \n\nActually if items is a single dict, write json.dumps(doc, indent=2) + \"\\n\". But is the existing single-object file one call or a wrapper with \"calls\"? `x[\"response\"][\"openrouter_metadata\"]` and `x[\"request\"][\"url\"]` in other tests suggest it's a single call object at top level. Then preserve.\n\nHmm, but what if it's JSONL already (multiple entries)? Then doc is None (ValueError) and we handle lines.\n\nHmm, but json.loads could succeed on a JSONL file? No.\n\nOK. But wait \u2014 will stamping the maker log create problems? The maker log's calls currently produce hand times with allow_hand; stamping them makes them logged. Cross-checks use `call.started`/`c