mit risk: The user said \"ONE defect, one file\" and \"Implement repair (ii)\" with 3 steps, and \"Do NOT delete any check\". They may not be aware of the wider fallout... but they claim running the suite shows the KeyError first (it crashes early, so they never saw the later failures). So after fixing the KeyError, they'd hit `bad-remedy` KeyError etc.\n\nTo be safe and thorough, my edit should make ALL cases pass. That means: introduce a logged base and switch every record-writing case onto it, while keeping all refusal cases on the unstamped fixture (still valid, since they only need exit 3 + tag).\n\nHmm, but risk: switching e.g. `same-family` to LOGGED might alter outcomes (e.g., `sameModelAsMaker` requires maker log model change...). Let's check each carefully.\n\nActually, maybe a lower-risk approach: rather than switching dozens of jobs, make `job_copy` itself return a *logged* copy? No \u2014 refusal cases rely on the unstamped log for their tags? No, they rely on other refusals; but T2/T2b/T2c/T2d/T3-contradiction/E3 DO rely on the unstamped log (T2 expects hand-time refusal naming harness? T2's \"T2c\" check needs disposition.at inputs-no-ledger \u2713 independent). E3 absolutely needs FIX unstamped. T2's first assertion: `rc == 3 and \"[hand-time]\" in out and \"REFUSED (history)\" in out and not o.exists()` \u2713 independent of log stamping. T2c: disposition.at inputs-no-ledger \u2713 independent.\n\nBut if job_copy returned logged copies, the *refusal* cases like \"no-maker-log\" (deletes maker log) \u2192 [time] \u2713; \"wrong-model\" \u2192 [model] \u2713; \"pin-late\" \u2192 [order] \u2713; \"fail-*\" overlays \u2192 tags \u2713; \"vendor-host\" \u2192 [endpoint] naming api.deepinfra.com \u2713; \"unlinked-control\" \u2192 needs a [time] refusal; with logged copies, ev_calls non-empty and `link_call` with call=\"eval_nope\" \u2192 [time] \"no harness call ... is linked\" \u2713 (better!). tol-61 \u2192 cross_check [time] \u2713.\n\nHmm, but the failure overlays (`failures/hand-times` etc.) also include typed pass times? They expect specific refusals; with logged copies they'd still fail for their reason \u2713 but maybe now *succeed* instead? E.g., `fail-after-emit` overlay expects [after-emit] \u2713 regardless.\n\nRisk: `agent-approval` overlay \u2713.\n\nBut this blanket change would also affect `job_copy` used for \"no-inputs\" (deletes custody.json) \u2713, and \"open-job\" (rc 2) \u2713.\n\nHowever, the E2 case (`t2-pass-at`) expects typed `at` to be a hand time (inputs-no-ledger) \u2014 works with logged copies \u2713, and expects rc == 0 with allow_hand and checks[0].at == \"2026-10-20T12:00:03Z\" \u2014 that requires the log's call time to be 12:00:03Z; with our stamping preserved instants \u2713 (assuming the fixture's server time is 12:00:03Z as the test states).\n\nBut blanket-changing `job_copy` alters the refusal tests in subtle ways I can't verify (e.g., \"model-approver\" expects [approval] \u2014 fine).\n\nHmm, but there's a real danger: `job_copy` is used by `refused(...)` heavily. If a job that previously refused *only* because of X now has an additional refusal, fine. But could enabling the evaluator log *remove* a refusal and make rc == 0? E.g. \"no-maker-log\"? no. \"unlinked-control\": previously (with read failing) [time] from the evaluator; now control-linking [time] \u2713 still refused.\n\nAlternatively, the minimal-risk approach: don't touch refusal cases; only change cases expecting a written record. That requires a per-call decision \u2014 many places. That's a big diff but surgical.\n\nLet me enumerate precisely from the file (line by line) which `emit(...)` calls expect a record (rc 0 or 1 with record read). I'll go through the custody part.\n\nA. emit-success block (FIX direct) \u2014 6 checks + refusal new.\nB. \"given\" job_copy \u2192 record.\nC. \"no-disposition\" \u2192 record.\nD. \"bad-remedy\" \u2192 record (unconditional read).\nE. \"no-violation-dimension\" \u2192 record.\nF. \"same-family\" \u2192 record.\nG. \"no-inputs\" \u2192 record; no evaluators \u21d2 no refusal; keep FIX (works). Actually `ci` = {} \u2192 no evaluators \u2192 no refusal \u2713.\nH. \"open-job\" \u2192 rc 2 \u2713.\nI. fail-* refusals \u2713.\nJ. keep-old \u2713.\nK. tol-61 refusal \u2713; tol-59 \u2192 record (needs base).\nL. vendor-host \u2713.\nM. pin-fb/pin-miss/pin-late/pin-attempt/pin-none/pin-unlogged refusals \u2713.\nN. \"pin-unpinned\" \u2192 record rc 1.\nO. \"pin-deny\" \u2192 rc 0.\nP. \"pin-nobody\" \u2192 record rc 1.\nQ. ledger-pin-late refusal \u2713 (uses run_py on ledger, not emit; FIX copy fine).\nR. ledger-pin-ok \u2192 ledger only \u2713.\nS. no-maker-log refusal \u2713; wrong-model \u2713; unlinked-control \u2713.\nT. approval refusals \u2713; agent-adopted \u2192 rc 0 (needs base).\nU. live-mismatch \u2713; live-match \u2192 rc 0 (base).\nV. bar-moved refusal \u2713.\nW. head/emitter revision check \u2192 uses `rec` (from logged base \u2713).\nX. exp-tree.json `emit(FIX, ...)` rc 0 \u2192 base.\nY. exp-bad refusal \u2713; dirty/nogit refusals \u2713.\nZ. bundle \u2192 uses FIX + rec_path: must align with the base.\nAA. T2 refusal \u2713 (FIX needed! T2 asserts hand-time refusal naming fields; would logged FIX still refuse? With LOGGED, hand times from maker gone, but disposition.at typed remains \u2192 [hand-time] \u2713. But keep FIX to be faithful: the T2 case is specifically \"hand times are refused by default\". Hmm, with FIX the refusal is now [time] from the eval log, but wait \u2014 the eval log mismatch... hold on. Which refusal happens with FIX? The [time] from read. But then hand_times never get marked (maker_history runs before evaluators! yes: maker loop runs first, marks maker hand times; then evaluators). So [hand-time] appears \u2713. And T2 asserts \"[hand-time]\" \u2713 and \"disposition.at\" with inputs-no-ledger \u2713 (disposition marking happens AFTER evaluators \u2014 it's near the end of build, after checks; careful: `mark(...)` for disposition happens later, and `if hand and not allow_hand: ref.add(\"hand-time\", ...)` happens at the very end of build \u2713 regardless of earlier refusals). Good \u2713.\n\nKeep T2 on FIX (it's a default-refusal test, and FIX's unstamped log is the worst case). \u2713\n\nBB. T2b/T2d on job_copy \u2192 refusal \u2713 keep.\nCC. T3 allow on FIX \u2192 needs base (LOGGED).\nDD. E2 (t2-pass-at) refusal + allow-record \u2192 the allow part needs base; the refusal part too? The refusal part asserts \"passes.pass-a.at\" and \"inputs-no-ledger\" \u2014 with LOGGED base, typed at still marked \u2713 and rc 3 \u2713. So use LOGGED for both.\nEE. E3 on FIX \u2192 keep (asserts the unstamped log's refusal).\nFF. E1 dispose-mismatch on job_copy + allow \u2192 expects [time] \"disposition:\" \u2713 either base; with LOGGED base, the dispose mismatch still refuses \u2713 and record not needed. Keep FIX? With FIX, [time] from log too; assertion `\"disposition:\" in out and \"[time]\" in out` \u2713. Keep as is.\nGG. roundtrip emit(FIX) rc 0 \u2192 base.\nHH. F1f \u2192 first rc 3 \u2713; second rc 0 \u2192 base LOGGED.\nII. f1h \u2192 record \u2192 base.\nJJ. x1 \u2192 record \u2192 base.\nKK. tool-built \u2192 uses job_copy \u2192 if I change job_copy's default base to LOGGED, the tool_built_job would strip times and stamp logs anyway \u2713 \u2014 actually it deletes `at`s and stamps, so it works on either base. Fine.\n\nNow \"P\" pin-nobody: it edits the maker log with `json.loads(...)` \u2192 expects a dict. On LOGGED, the maker log: we preserve dict form \u2713 (must ensure). And the evaluator log gets stamped \u2713.\n\nAnd \"M\" pin-attempt reads `json.loads(lg.read_text())` as dict \u2014 must preserve dict form for maker log. \u2713 with preservation.\n\nAlso \"F\" same-family does `ml.write_text(ml.read_text().replace(...))` \u2713.\n\nNow, how to give the tests a LOGGED base with minimal, readable edits:\n\nDefine near `job_copy`:\n\n```python\ndef logger_stamped(name):\n    \"\"\"A copy of the job-rejected fixture whose harness logs carry the logger's own sent_at and\n    received_at on every call -- the entry shape tests/test_evaluator_harness_log.py D1-D3 asserts\n    (sent_at, received_at, request.url, response.model). The fixture's logs carry server times only,\n    so a MODEL evaluator's check times have no logged source and the emitter REFUSES the record\n    (D6/D7); a case that needs a WRITTEN record runs on this copy instead. The instants are the ones\n    the fixture already carries (the response `created` epoch, else its Date header), so nothing the\n    fixture asserts about its own timeline moves.\"\"\"\n    d = TMP / name\n    shutil.copytree(FIX, d)\n    for lg in sorted((d / \"custody\" / \"evidence\").glob(\"harness-*.log\")):\n        _stamp_logger_times(lg)\n    return d\n\n\ndef _stamp_logger_times(path):\n    text = path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    try:\n        doc = json.loads(text)\n    except ValueError:\n        doc = None\n    if isinstance(doc, list):\n        items, dump = doc, lambda it: json.dumps(it, indent=2) + \"\\n\"\n    elif isinstance(doc, dict):\n        items, dump = [doc], lambda it: json.dumps(it[0], indent=2) + \"\\n\"\n    else:\n        items = [json.loads(ln) for ln in text.splitlines() if ln.strip()]\n        dump = lambda it: \"\".join(json.dumps(e) + \"\\n\" for e in it)\n    for e in items:\n        if not isinstance(e, dict) or e.get(\"sent_at\") or e.get(\"received_at\"):\n            continue\n        at = _server_instant(e)\n        if at:\n            e[\"sent_at\"] = e[\"received_at\"] = at\n    path.write_text(dump(items), encoding=\"utf-8\")\n\n\ndef _server_instant(e):\n    \"\"\"The instant the fixture's response carried: its `created` epoch, else its Date header.\"\"\"\n    created = (e.get(\"response\") or {}).get(\"created\")\n    if isinstance(created, (int, float)) and not isinstance(created, bool):\n        return datetime.fromtimestamp(created, timezone.utc).strftime(\"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ\")\n    hdrs = e.get(\"response_headers\") or e.get(\"headers\") or (e.get(\"response\") or {}).get(\"headers\") or {}\n    date = hdrs.get(\"Date\") or hdrs.get(\"date\")\n    if isinstance(date, str) and date:\n        try:\n            from email.utils import parsedate_to_datetime\n            parsed = parsedate_to_datetime(date)\n        except (TypeError, ValueError):\n            return None\n        if parsed.tzinfo is None:\n            parsed = parsed.replace(tzinfo=timezone.utc)\n        return parsed.astimezone(timezone.utc).strftime(\"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ\")\n    return None\n```\n\nThen LOGGED = logger_stamped(\"fixture-logged\") \u2014 but careful: it's created at module scope in the custody section, like SNAP. But `TMP` blocks are removed at the end (`shutil.rmtree(TMP)`). Fine.\n\nWhere does `FIX` exist? Defined at top of PART 3. \u2713. And `datetime`/`timezone` imported there \u2713 (`from datetime import datetime, timedelta, timezone`).\n\nNow, `job_copy(name, overlay)` \u2014 used by many. I'll add a `base` parameter? Many call sites. Alternative: define\n\n```python\ndef success_job(name, overlay=None):\n    \"\"\"job_copy, but with harness logs that carry the logger's own times (a record can be written).\"\"\"\n    d = TMP / name\n    shutil.copytree(LOGGED, d)\n    if overlay:\n        shutil.copytree(FIX.parent / \"failures\" / overlay, d, dirs_exist_ok=True)\n    return d\n```\n\nThen change the ~10 record-writing call sites to `success_job`. Hmm, but \"same-family\", \"pin-deny\", etc. use `job_copy`. Let me count edits. Each is a small SEARCH/REPLACE.\n\nHmm, that's ~12 blocks. Plus the emit-success block, E2/T3, roundtrip, exp-tree, f1h, x1, agent-adopted, live-match, pin-unpinned, pin-nobody, pin-deny, tol-59, given, no-disposition, bad-remedy, no-violation-dimension, same-family, F1f(second).\n\nAlternatively \u2014 simpler and fewer edits: change `job_copy` to copy from LOGGED *by default*, and give the refusal-focused ones... no, they're fine either way; but E3/T2/T2b need FIX explicitly (they use FIX directly, not job_copy!). Let me check: E3 uses `emit(FIX, ...)` \u2713 direct. T2 `emit(FIX, ...)` \u2713. T2b/T2d use job_copy \u2192 with LOGGED base, would T2b still refuse with \"ledger-hand\"? Yes: appends a hand-written approve line \u2192 [hand-time] ledger-hand \u2713. T2d \u2713. tol-61 \u2713. vendor-host \u2713. fail-* overlays: `job_copy(f\"fail-{ov}\", ov)` \u2014 with LOGGED base + overlay \u2713 still refuse (their causes are in inputs/logs). pin-late overlay etc \u2713. no-orchestrator \u2713.\n\nRisk of blanket change: something that previously refused now emits. Consider \"unlinked-control\": LOGGED + control call \"eval_nope\" \u2192 link_call refuses [time] \u2713. \"wrong-model\" \u2713. \"no-maker-log\" \u2713.\n\nConsider \"fail-hand-times\" overlay: expects [time]; with LOGGED, the typed pass/control times in the overlay conflict with the log \u2192 [time] \u2713.\n\nConsider \"pin-unpinned\" overlay expecting rc == 1 with a record: with LOGGED \u2713 (currently fails, needs base).\n\nConsider \"agent-adopted\" rc 0 \u2713.\n\nSo blanket-changing `job_copy` to base on LOGGED solves all record-writing sites at once, and refusal sites keep refusing. That's ~1 edit instead of 12. But: does it break \"no-inputs\"? copytree(LOGGED) then delete custody.json \u2713 same. \"open-job\" \u2713.\n\nBut wait: two refusal cases may become *writes*? Let's scan each `refused(...)` / rc==3 expectation:\n\n1. fail-hand-times/after-emit/router-endpoint/agent-approval \u2014 causes in overlay inputs/logs. For \"router-endpoint\" (overlay), the maker log presumably has a router URL where upstream can't be determined \u2192 [endpoint] \u2713.\n2. keep-old (agent-approval) \u2713.\n3. tol-61 \u2713.\n4. vendor-host \u2713.\n5. pin-fb \u2713 pin-miss \u2713 pin-late \u2713 pin-attempt \u2713 pin-none \u2713 pin-unlogged (no events log; typed pin \u2192 [endpoint]) \u2713.\n6. ledger-pin-late: run_py on ledger \u2713 (not emit).\n7. no-maker-log \u2713 wrong-model \u2713 unlinked-control \u2713.\n8. approval ones \u2713.\n9. live-mismatch \u2713 (appends conflicting ledger line).\n10. bar-moved \u2713.\n11. exp-bad, dirty, nogit \u2713.\n12. T2b/T2d \u2713.\n13. \"t3-contradiction\" (failures/hand-times with allow_hand) \u2713 expects rc 3.\n14. E1 dispose-mismatch: appends a dispose line + typed at \u2192 [time] \"disposition:\" \u2713.\n15. F1f first \u2713 ([approval]).\n\nAnd T3 on FIX \u2192 change to LOGGED.\n\nHmm, but one more: with LOGGED base, the evaluator's log now parses, and `ev_entries` populated \u2192 checks get `at` from entries; `check_order` compares producedAt < check times; the times are the fixture's own \u2192 previously (with entries failing) the check's `at` came from `call.at` (same). Wait, before the fix (old emitter) the check's `at` came from `link_call`'s call.at and `x.at_source` from `call.source_at()`. In the fixed emitter, entries override: `at_v = H.iso(entry.received_at)` and `xc[\"at_source\"] = entry.source`. Since entry.received_at == call.at (our stamping) \u2713 same.\n\nAlright. But is `harness_log.read`'s `received_at` strictly from the entry dict? yes.\n\nOne more risk: `harness_log.read` may demand `call` names matching, or may reject `sent_at == received_at` (D3 says received must not be BEFORE sent; equal ok \u2713).\n\nAnother risk: read might require the log file to be a JSON LIST. If the eval log is a dict (single call), then... E3's `count(\"harness-eval-a.log#\") >= 3` implies 3+ entries. So it's a list or JSONL. Our stamper handles both.\n\nNow the emit-success block rewrite:\n\n```python\n# the fixture's own harness logs carry server times only: a MODEL evaluator whose check times have no\n# logged source REFUSES the record (D6/D7, tests/test_evaluator_harness_log.py) -- it must not reach a\n# record at all. The emit-success checks run on a copy whose calls carry the logger's own times.\no_ref = TMP / \"fixture-refused.json\"\nrc, out = emit(FIX, o_ref)\nres((f\"{S} \u00b7 a model evaluator whose harness log carries no logger times is refused, naming the log \"\n     f\"(exit 3, [time], nothing written)\",\n     rc == 3 and \"[time]\" in out and \"harness\" in out and not o_ref.exists(), f\"exit {rc}: {out.strip()[-140:]}\"))\n\nLOGGED = logger_stamped(\"fixture-logged\")   # ... maybe define earlier\nrec_path = TMP / \"fixture.custody.json\"\nrc, out = emit(LOGGED, rec_path)\nrec = ...\nres((f\"{S} \u00b7 fixture job with logger-stamped harness logs emits with no gap (exit 0)\", rc == 0, out.strip()[-90:]))\n```\n\nThen bundle uses LOGGED.\n\nWhere to define LOGGED? Right before use in the emit-success block (module level).\n\nThen T3: `emit(LOGGED, o, allow_hand=True)`.\n\nE2: `job_copy(\"t2-pass-at\")` \u2192 with blanket change it's LOGGED-based \u2713.\n\nRoundtrip: `emit(LOGGED, rt_rec, allow_hand=True)` and `verify_record(rt_rec, LOGGED, VCO)`.\n\nexp-tree: `emit(LOGGED, TMP/\"exp-tree.json\", extra=(\"--expect-revision\", head[1][:12]))` \u2014 but `head` computed from SNAP; the record's emitter commit is from SNAP \u2713 (emit runs SNAP's emitter with a LOGGED job dir). \u2713\n\nBundle: `run_py([... \"pack_stranger_bundle.py\", \"--job-dir\", str(LOGGED), \"--record\", str(rec_path), ...])` and the determinism second run likewise.\n\nHmm, wait: the bundle test also checks `need` includes \"bar/bar.md\", \"files/artifact.md\", \"evidence/pass-a-D2.txt\", \"evidence/harness-eval-a.log\" \u2014 present in LOGGED \u2713.\n\nAnd `res(... record carries no file the fixture lacks ...)` uses FIX/bar.md \u2713 (same file in copy).\n\nNow also `res((f\"{S} \u00b7 the record names the emitter's commit and tree, clean\", rec...` uses rec from LOGGED \u2713.\n\nNow, \"the endpoint refusal names the upstream the log shows\" etc. unchanged.\n\nNow: does `emit(LOGGED, ...)` produce `x-plumbline.times` \"harness log\"? For the maker, `mk.get(\"startedAt\")` now from log (sent_at) \u2713.\n\nPotential problem: `H.cross_check(ref, \"startedAt\", ci.get(\"startedAt\"), started, ...)`: started = first.started = sent_at = created instant = same as before \u2713.\n\nPotential problem: order check `before(\"bar.frozenAt\", F, \"startedAt\", S, strict=True)` \u2713 unchanged.\n\nPotential problem: `check_order` `before(\"producedAt\", Pd, n, t)` for checks \u2014 unchanged \u2713.\n\nAnother potential problem: `spend` \u2014 unchanged.\n\nOK. Also: with LOGGED, `hand_times` for T3 include `disposition.at` \u2713 and maybe nothing else \u2014 `len(ht) >= 1` \u2713.\n\nHmm, but T3 also asserts `by_field[\"disposition.at\"].get(\"line\") is not None` \u2713 (typed in custody.json \u2192 line found).\n\nNow: think about whether `emit(LOGGED, ...)` might exit 1 (hard gap) for some reason. Previously FIX emitted exit 0; the only changes are log times. Hand-time refusals disappear (not gifts). Hard gaps: `routing.maker.request`? The fixture's request asks for the pin properly (the \"matching pin\" test asserts request_ok True) \u2713. So exit 0 \u2713.\n\nWait, careful: previously with hand times allowed, `hand` dict entries existed; exit code unaffected. \u2713\n\nNow, another thought: `hard` gaps include missing `bar.approvals` etc. \u2014 unchanged.\n\nOK. Also the initial `rc, out = emit(FIX, rec_path)` removal: rec_path used later (bundle, V1). We keep the name.\n\nNow, one important detail: the refusal asserted for FIX \u2014 `\"[time]\" in out`. Is the tag definitely \"time\"? In the given emitter, `ref.add(\"time\", ...)` for HarnessLogError \u2713. But if the refusal path is `find_log` returning None \u2192 also \"time\" \u2713. Good.\n\nDo we also want to assert the *content* \"harness\"? Both messages contain \"harness log\" except the read error which contains the log name \"harness-eval-a.log\" \u2192 contains \"harness\" \u2713.\n\nAlso \"nothing written\" \u2713.\n\nNow: