ped ones (approvals, disposition). For T3 `len(ht) >= 1` \u2713.\n\nHmm, but T3 also asserts `by_field[\"disposition.at\"].get(\"line\") is not None` \u2014 depends on `line_in(inputs_path, at, \"at\")` finding the line in custody.json \u2713 (typed there).\n\nWait, actually: is disposition.at typed in FIX's custody.json? T2c asserts disposition.at is named as inputs-no-ledger \u2713 yes.\n\nNow: E1 dispose-mismatch is `job_copy(\"dispose-mismatch\")` \u2192 LOGGED base; appends a dispose line with decided_at 12:31 while custody.json has at 12:30 \u2192 [time] \u2713.\n\nHmm, but with LOGGED, the record... refusal anyway \u2713.\n\nNow consider \"live-match\": appends matching freeze+approve lines \u2192 expects rc 0 and `rl[\"bar\"][\"approvals\"][1][\"x-plumbline\"][\"time_source\"].startswith(\"logged live\")` \u2713.\n\n\"agent-adopted\" rc 0 \u2713.\n\n\"f1h\": rc 0 \u2713 \u2014 but wait, it appends ledger lines with `writer: kit/custody_ledger.py@...` and times 2026-10-20T10:xx. `check_order` may now complain about order? The times are 10:30/10:32 etc. vs check times 12:00 \u2713.\n\nHmm, and `spend_block` uses calls \u2713.\n\n\"x1-inputs\" rc 0 \u2713.\n\nNow: does any of these now produce a hard gap? They already produced exit 0/1 as asserted \u2713.\n\nWait, one: \"given\" expects r2 verdict \"reject\" \u2014 `xp[\"verdict\"]` from the \u00a75 record \u2713 unchanged.\n\nOK, also: `disposition` \"given\" \u2014 `state` = packet's \u2713.\n\nNow let me reconsider Edit 1's placement and whether defining LOGGED before `job_copy` is fine w.r.t. name resolution: yes.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: `logger_stamped` uses `_stamp_logger_times` and `_server_instant` defined just above \u2713.\n\nAdditionally, do we need `from email.utils import parsedate_to_datetime` at module top? I'll import locally inside the function to keep the diff small (imports inside functions are OK but ruff might complain; fine).\n\nActually, better: put the import at the top-level import block? The existing top has `import hashlib, json, os, subprocess, sys` and PART 3 does `import shutil, tempfile, zipfile, copy, hashlib, time` and `from datetime import ...`. Adding `from email.utils import parsedate_to_datetime` to the PART-3 import line is clean:\n\n```python\nimport shutil, tempfile, zipfile, copy, hashlib, time\nfrom datetime import datetime, timedelta, timezone\nfrom email.utils import parsedate_to_datetime\n```\n\nThen edit that line. Yes, do that.\n\nNow let me write `_server_instant` without local import.\n\nNow, potential lint: lambda assignment (E731) \u2014 the file already does similar? `stamp_log` uses conditional expression with `dump`? It uses inline ternaries. To avoid E731, I'll structure differently:\n\n```python\ndef _stamp_logger_times(path):\n    text = path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    try:\n        doc = json.loads(text)\n    except ValueError:\n        doc = None\n    array = isinstance(doc, list)\n    single = isinstance(doc, dict)\n    items = doc if array else ([doc] if single else [json.loads(ln) for ln in text.splitlines() if ln.strip()])\n    for e in items:\n        if isinstance(e, dict) and not e.get(\"sent_at\") and not e.get(\"received_at\"):\n            at = _server_instant(e)\n            if at:\n                e[\"sent_at\"] = e[\"received_at\"] = at\n    if array:\n        path.write_text(json.dumps(items, indent=2) + \"\\n\", encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    elif single:\n        path.write_text(json.dumps(items[0], indent=2) + \"\\n\", encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    else:\n        path.write_text(\"\".join(json.dumps(e) + \"\\n\" for e in items), encoding=\"utf-8\")\n```\n\nGood.\n\nNow write `logger_stamped`:\n\n```python\ndef logger_stamped(name):\n    \"\"\"A copy of the job-rejected fixture whose harness logs carry the logger's own sent_at and received_at on\n    every call -- the entry shape the frozen bar asserts (tests/test_evaluator_harness_log.py D1-D3: sent_at,\n    received_at, request.url, response.mode