p()[-140:]}\"))\n\n# the emitter on the synthetic fixture, every call carrying the logger's own times\nrec_path = TMP / \"fixture.custody.json\"\nrc, out = emit(LOGGED, rec_path)\nrec = json.loads(rec_path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\")) if rec_path.is_file() else {}\nres((f\"{S} \u00b7 fixture job with logger-stamped harness logs emits with no gap (exit 0)\", rc == 0, out.strip()[-90:]))\n```\n\nGood.\n\nNow the message to the user: explain changes briefly and note I could not run the suite here (no shell execution available? Actually I'm the assistant; I can't run). The user asked to run both and report numbers \u2014 I cannot execute commands. I should say so and ask them to run. Hmm, \"Reply in English\", they expect code. I'll note I can't execute and give the commands.\n\nLet me double check block 2 text exactly matches, including the blank lines around. The file:\n\n```\ndef job_copy(name, overlay=None):\n    d = TMP / name\n    shutil.copytree(FIX, d)\n    if overlay:\n        shutil.copytree(FIX.parent / \"failures\" / overlay, d, dirs_exist_ok=True)\n    return d\n\n\ndef append_text(path, text):\n```\n\nOK.\n\nNow, for `emit(LOGGED, o, allow_hand=True)` in T3 \u2014 the SEARCH must be unique. The line `o = TMP / \"t3-allow.json\"` is unique \u2713.\n\nWrite everything.\n\nOne more check on `_server_instant` returning e.g. \"2026-10-20T12:00:03Z\" \u2014 from `created` epoch: `datetime.fromtimestamp(created, timezone.utc)`. If the fixture's created epoch is float, includes microseconds \u2192 format omits them \u2713 (that's fine; microsecond truncation could shift by <1s; RFC regex in emitter allows optional fraction; our string has none \u2713 and cross_check tolerance 60s \u2713).\n\nBut careful: if `created` has fractional seconds and the typed `at` is exactly the integer second, fine.\n\nAlso H.iso format: \"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ\"? presumably \u2713 (matches the emitter's RFC regex).\n\nNow, `_stamp_logger_times` for the JSONL branch: `json.loads(ln)` may fail on blank? filtered \u2713.\n\nEdge: file may be JSONL but with a leading BOM? ignore.\n\nLet me finalize. Also should I worry that `logger_stamped`'s `for lg in ...glob(\"harness-*.log\")` matches `harness-maker-fixture.log` \u2713 and `harness-eval-a.log` \u2713. Are there others (e.g. controls logs)? glob covers all \u2713.\n\nAlso: should we stamp logs only under evidence? yes.\n\nWrite blocks now.\n\nAlso the `check` import of datetime in `_server_instant` uses `datetime.fromtimestamp` \u2014 `datetime` is imported from datetime module \u2713.\n\nFinal answer with blocks and a note about not being able to execute."}, "reasoning_content": "Let me understand the situation. We need to edit `tests/validate_schemas.py` only.\n\nThe FIX fixture (`tests/fixtures/custody/job-rejected`) has a model evaluator and no harness log; under the new contract (D6/D7) the emitter refuses it. Actually wait: the E3 test asserts that `emit(FIX, ...)` refuses with hand-time for the evaluator log calls (harness-eval-a.log has entries without logger sent_at). And there's `custody/evidence/harness-eval-a.log` present in the fixture (stranger bundle needs it). So the evaluator has a harness log but the log entries lack sent_at/received_at \u2192 hand times \u2192 refusal exit 3.\n\nHmm, but the user says \"The fixture job at FIX has a MODEL evaluator and no harness log.\" Let me check: `emit(FIX, rec_path)` with `allow_hand=True` (default of emit is allow_hand=True). So hand times are allowed, record gets written. But D6/D7 says a model evaluator with no harness log refuses. Hmm \u2014 does FIX have `harness_log` named in custody inputs? Let me check the fixture's custody.json content... We don't have it. The stranger bundle test requires `evidence/harness-eval-a.log` present in the zip. So there is an evaluator harness log.\n\nBut the user's description says no harness log. Perhaps the emitted harness log exists but the evaluator's `harness_log` field is not named in custody inputs? Actually `find_log` for evaluator uses `e.get(\"harness_log\")` or the CL1-15 external entry. Hmm.\n\nRegardless, the instruction is: the emitter now refuses FIX. Well, we can't run it. But the user asserts: \"The fixture job at FIX has a MODEL evaluator and no harness log. Under the new contract (D6/D7) the emitter REFUSES it, so rec is {} and line 510 raises KeyError: 'predicate'.\"\n\nHmm, but wait: if FIX's model evaluator had no harness log, then `mark` of hand times... whatever. The important thing: we need to make FIX assert refusal, and move emit-success checks to a fixture that supplies harness log times.\n\nWhich fixture supplies real logger times? Perhaps `tests/fixtures/custody/failures/...`? Or maybe the tool-built job (TJ) which stamps logs via `stamp_log`. Actually in the file, `tool_built_job` builds a job with stamped logs and then emits with `allow_hand=False` and asserts exit 0. That's inside a try/except at the end (T4 case) \u2014 an existing fixture. But we need one for the emit-success checks early in the file (before `tool_built_job` is defined at line ~700).\n\nAlternatively, we could build the emit-success fixture locally: copy FIX, and stamp its harness logs with logger times (like `stamp_log`). But the emitter also requires hand times elsewhere \u2014 with `allow_hand=True` (as `emit` default), hand times are allowed, so only the missing-log refusal matters. Also, we need an \"emits with no gap (exit 0)\" \u2014 exit 0 means no hard gaps. That's more demanding: all fields must have sources.\n\nHmm, does the current FIX emit with exit 0? Yes \u2014 currently `res(... fixture job emits with no gap (exit 0), rc == 0)`. So FIX emits with 0 gaps. So FIX has everything: bar, approvals, maker model with harness log, evaluator with harness log... Wait, but if the evaluator had no harness log, the emitter would add a gap \"no harness log (CL1-15) for model evaluator\" (soft, hard=False). Hmm, that gap is `hard=False` (external gap), so exit 0 still possible.\n\nActually now under the new contract, a model evaluator with no harness log REFUSES (exit 3). So FIX must have a harness log for the evaluator... but then the log's calls lack logger sent_at \u2192 in `evaluator_history`, `harness_log.read(log)` is called first, which raises HarnessLogError if entries are missing sent_at/received_at or reversed \u2192 refusal with kind \"time\". So FIX refuses now, regardless of allow_hand. That matches the user's statement loosely (\"no harness log\" is their description, but really the log is unreadable per the new module).\n\nOK so what fixture supplies real logger times? Let me think: `tests/fixtures/custody/` \u2014 we've seen FIX = job-rejected, plus `failures/` overlays. Is there another fixture? The user says \"There is already a fixture in the repo that supplies harness-log times: kit/emit_custody.py's expectations and the corpus battery's own fixtures, plus the shape the frozen bar builds at tests/test_evaluator_harness_log.py \u2014 read that bar for the exact harness-log entry shape (sent_at, received_at, request.url, response.model) and reuse it.\"\n\nHmm, that's a bit vague. I think the cleanest approach: create a helper that copies FIX and rewrites its harness logs with logger-stamped entries, then use that as the emit-success fixture. That satisfies \"reuse the shape\" from the bar.\n\nBut careful: rewriting the evaluator harness log's entries means the entries must contain `sent_at`, `received_at`, `request.url`, `response.model`, and `call` matching the pass's `call` field in custody inputs. The `read()` function in kit/evaluator_harness_log.py we only have a summary \u2014 `read(path) -> dict`, and `HarnessLogError`. The tests in the frozen bar show entries: `{\"call\": \"dim1#p1\", \"sent_at\": ..., \"received_at\": ..., \"request\": {\"url\": ...}, \"response\": {\"model\": ...}}`. And entries keyed by call id. The record takes check times from `ev_entries[evaluator][f.get(\"call\")]` \u2192 `entry.received_at`, `entry.source`, `entry.name`.\n\nSo for the emit-success fixture: rewrite each call in the evaluator log, adding `sent_at`/`received_at`. But the existing log entries must already have `call` names matching custody inputs' passes[].call and controls[].call. Since FIX currently emits with exit 0 (with hand-time allowance), the `x-plumbline` cross-checks pass, meaning times in custody inputs vs log server times agree within tolerance. If we add sent_at/received_at, then `H.cross_check` compares custody's typed `at` with `call.at` which now = received_at. So we must set received_at equal to the custody `at` (or within 60s). Safer: set sent_at = at - 4s (or at - 1s) and received_at = at, matching the custody inputs' times. But we don't know the fixture content precisely.\n\nHmm, alternatively set sent_at = received_at = the entry's existing server time (Date header / created). That's what `stamp_log` does at the end of the file: it uses the same `at` for every call, from the freeze time + 1 second. Hmm, but that's for the tool-built job.\n\nRisk: the fixture's server-side times may be consistent with custody `at` values already (since currently hand `at` matches server time). Then setting sent_at = received_at = server time would keep cross-check passing (at == received_at). Good. And order checks: check times must be before disposition.at, after producedAt (maker last call). Hmm, if the maker log also gets stamped, the maker's producedAt is the last call's received_at = server Date header. There's already a cross_check between custody's producedAt and log's produced, which currently passes (as hand time allowed? cross_check happens regardless of hand allowance \u2014 actually `H.cross_check(ref, \"producedAt\", ci.get(\"producedAt\"), produced, ...)` \u2014 refusals don't depend on allow_hand). So currently the fixture's typed producedAt matches the log's times. Good, so the fixture is self-consistent already.\n\nSo simply adding `sent_at`/`received_a