rue) \u2713. So exit 0 \u2713.\n\nWait, careful: previously with hand times allowed, `hand` dict entries existed; exit code unaffected. \u2713\n\nNow, another thought: `hard` gaps include missing `bar.approvals` etc. \u2014 unchanged.\n\nOK. Also the initial `rc, out = emit(FIX, rec_path)` removal: rec_path used later (bundle, V1). We keep the name.\n\nNow, one important detail: the refusal asserted for FIX \u2014 `\"[time]\" in out`. Is the tag definitely \"time\"? In the given emitter, `ref.add(\"time\", ...)` for HarnessLogError \u2713. But if the refusal path is `find_log` returning None \u2192 also \"time\" \u2713. Good.\n\nDo we also want to assert the *content* \"harness\"? Both messages contain \"harness log\" except the read error which contains the log name \"harness-eval-a.log\" \u2192 contains \"harness\" \u2713.\n\nAlso \"nothing written\" \u2713.\n\nNow: the order of definitions. `logger_stamped` defined after `job_copy`, and LOGGED created at the emit-success block. But `job_copy` (blanket changed) references LOGGED at call time \u2014 and `job_copy` is called earlier? Let's check: `job_copy` first used at `j = job_copy(\"given\")` which is after the emit-success block \u2713. And `SNAP`/`rec` etc. But `job_copy` is also used inside... no earlier. \u2713\n\nBut careful: with blanket change, `job_copy` uses LOGGED \u2192 recursion? No.\n\nHmm, but is the blanket change too clever? It changes semantics of `job_copy` for ALL tests, and if a refusal test's expected *specific* text was previously provided by the evaluator-log failure... e.g. \"T7b/E3\" uses FIX directly \u2713. Others: `refused(\"a model maker with no harness log\", j, \"time\")` \u2014 no text check \u2713.\n\nHmm, `refused(\"a hand time 61 s off its harness call (tolerance 60 s)\", j, \"time\")` \u2713.\n\nI think it's acceptable. But the diff for job_copy needs a comment explaining it, and keep a way to get the un-stamped copy (FIX directly).\n\nAlternatively, keep `job_copy` as-is and 