s fixed and committed, and presumably T4 passes now? They didn't run validate_schemas successfully (crash at line 510). But they might have. Whatever.\n\nThe strongest signal: T4 stamps logs and expects exit 0. Our approach mirrors `stamp_log` exactly. \n\nGiven that, maybe I should just reuse `stamp_log`! But it's defined later in the file (inside the T4 section, before `tool_built_job`), and takes a single `at`. Our `logger_stamped` could call it if moved up. Moving `_iso`/`_ts`/`stamp_log` up is possible: they're at module level, defined before `tool_built_job`, in the T4 area. Moving them up to the custody helper area would be a \"2-block move\" (delete + insert). That reduces duplicated parsing logic... but stamp_log takes one fixed `at` for all calls, which would flatten the timeline and break cross-checks (typed `at`s in custody.json for passes/controls vs a flat time). Wait \u2014 does the fixture type `at` for passes? E2 shows FIX has no typed passes at (they add it). Controls: `refused(\"...\")`... there's a control `call` linkage; controls likely have no typed `at` either (E2 only mentions passes). And approvals/freeze/disposition are typed. So a flat stamp might still pass cross-checks if passes/controls have no typed times... but `H.cross_check` also runs for `startedAt`/`producedAt` if typed in custody.json \u2014 are they? `tool_built_job` pops them, implying they ARE typed in the fixture. Then a flat stamp would break the startedAt cross-check \u2192 refusal.\n\nSo per-call instants are needed. Also T4 avoids this by removing typed times.\n\nTherefore implement our own stamper preserving each call's instant. Good.\n\nNow \u2014 hmm, one more thought about `_server_instant` precedence: `H.Call.at` = received or created or Date. `started` = sent or created or Date. So for `at`, if `received` absent \u2192 created, else Date. Using created-then-Date matches \u2713. \n\nBut careful: some entries may have neither created nor Date (then read would already fail for them: \"no time\"). Those would remain unstamped \u2192 refusal. Can't help; but E3 says each call has a server time source.\n\nLet me also double check `Call.hand_time_entries` sources: \"server-header\" and \"response-created\" correspond to Date and created \u2713.\n\nNow, about `external` entries in custody.json referencing `harness-eval-a.log` with a digest? The emitter computes the digest itself from the file, so no stale digest in inputs \u2713 (T5 not present here).\n\nAlright, let me now write the edits.\n\nEdit 1: job_copy base + helpers (insert `logger_stamped`, `_stamp_logger_times`, `_server_instant`).\n\nCurrent code:\n\n```python\ndef job_copy(name, overlay=None):\n    d = TMP / name\n    shutil.copytree(FIX, d)\n    if overlay:\n        shutil.copytree(FIX.parent / \"failures\" / overlay, d, dirs_exist_ok=True)\n    return d\n```\n\nReplace with new version + helpers. But `logger_stamped` uses `LOGGED`? No. And job_copy default base uses LOGGED which is defined later \u2014 fine at call time, but flake8-ish; acceptable. Hmm, but \"LOGGED\" must be defined before the first call. Let me instead define LOGGED immediately after the helpers:\n\n```python\nLOGGED = logger_stamped(\"fixture-logged\")\n```\n\nBut then the emit-success block uses LOGGED; and job_copy default base=LOGGED. Defining LOGGED right there is clean (before job_copy definition? order: helpers, LOGGED, job_copy). But LOGGED creation needs TMP \u2713.\n\nWait, but the emit-success block is where the fixture refusal happens; LOGGED creation causes an extra copytree \u2014 fine.\n\nEdit 2: emit-success block rewrite.\n\nCurrent:\n\n```python\n# the emitter on the synthetic fixture\nrec_path = TMP / \"fixture.custody.json\"\nrc, out = emit(FIX, rec_path)\nrec = json.loads(rec_path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\")) if rec_path.is_file() else {}\nres((f\"{S} \u00b7 fixture job emits with no gap (exit 0)\", rc == 0, out.strip()[-90:]))\ncheck(S, \"emitted record is valid against the PIN